  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nThe latest satellite imagery reveals a potent shortwave moving \neast through the Upper Midwest, resulting in a robust area of\nmainly elevated convection just west of Lake Michigan early \nthis morning. Surface observations and the SPC mesoanalysis \ndepict a tight moisture gradient extending roughly NW to SE \nalong the southern flank of this convection from just north of \nMilwaukee southeastwards towards the Mid-Atlantic Coast. The \nlatest radar imagery and trends suggest that this area of \nconvection may persist and become surface-based as it enters the\nLower Great Lakes region and Upper Ohio Valley later this \nmorning and early afternoon. \n\nDamaging wind gusts appear to be the primary concern with this \nfeature, given a strong DCAPE gradient in place across the \nregion and anticipated line storm mode, though some instances of\nlarge hail also cannot be ruled out, especially out ahead of \nthe complex. Given this, looking like an earlier time window \nfor the severe weather potential today, roughly between 10 AM\nand 3 PM as storms generally move east to southeast, with the\narea of concern encompassing more of Northwest and North-Central\nOhio rather than far Northeast Ohio and Northwest Pennsylvania. \n\nAttention then turns towards later this evening and overnight as\nanother round of nocturnal convection may develop across \nMichigan and move east to southeast into the area, though quite \na bit of uncertainty remains regarding this potential. The\nlatest guidance does suggest that most of this activity would \nremain elevated, though isolated large hail, gusty winds, and \nheavy rain cannot be ruled out. The frontal boundary will\nfinally be pushed south through the area on Saturday with\nisolated to scattered showers and thunderstorms possible, mainly\nalong and south of US-30. Severe weather is not expected at \nthis time.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... \nHigh pressure will begin to build south across the Great Lakes \nlate Saturday and persist into early next week. The latest \ntrends favor seasonable and mainly dry weather during this \ntimeframe with highs in the upper 70s to lower 80s and less \nhumidity. \n\nWill then begin watching the next system by mid-next week as\nanother upper-level ridge builds east across the Central and\nEastern CONUS, followed by the passage of a strong upper-level\ntrough moving east through the Upper Great Lakes. Shower and\nthunderstorms chances will increase beginning Wednesday \nafternoon and evening, with the potential continuing into\nThursday.",
